abstract: >-
  Pre-registered R analysis of CYB5R3 in hepatocellular carcinoma using TCGA-LIHC
  and GSE14520. The pre-registered hypothesis, that Hoshida molecular subclass
  explains the discordance between histologic grade and survival, is rejected on
  all four counts. Labeled post hoc analyses then show that grade is not
  prognostic in TCGA-LIHC and that the grade trend tracks proliferation, and that
  proliferation negatively confounds the association between CYB5R3 and survival.
  That finding is tested in GSE14520 under a rule fixed in advance. Version 1.1.0
  adds a clinical robustness stage, with alternative endpoints, liver function
  sensitivity and a bootstrap interval for the change in hazard ratio, and a
  referee stage that measures how much of that change is arithmetic rather than
  causal, because the Cox hazard ratio is not collapsible. That measurement is
  made by simulating from the fitted model with the proliferation score permuted,
  so that the covariate carries its prognostic strength but no association with
  the exposure. The referee stage also adds a baseline characteristics table,
  median follow-up by reverse Kaplan-Meier, a grade trend sensitivity check that
  removes the twelve grade 4 tumors, ESTIMATE stromal and immune scores, and a
  HALLMARK_G2M_CHECKPOINT proliferation score. This repository contains the
  decision rules, the analysis scripts, the frozen subclass templates, every
  result file, the run logs and the manuscript figures.
